{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:19:57.306Z","ordinal":7946,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a04adc-934d-7aa3-b352-bdba70d0f67c","turn_id":"01a05f0e-9da8-7d33-a946-e7f5d5e0a58a","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_066dfde3c06b8029016a974f8a306087d0a2005cfd8786d9be","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"I agree with Claude’s assessment. The architecture and implementation appear substantively strong; the remaining concern is process traceability.\n\nI would not automatically mark the specs “accepted” merely because code now exists. Instead, do one explicit acceptance pass:\n\n- Review each 007–013 spec against the implemented behavior and tests.\n- Record any intentional deviations in each spec’s revision note.\n- Mark them `accepted` only after that review, with the acceptance date/reviewer.\n- Update the implementation plan to link the accepted specs and implementation commits.\n\nThat preserves the important distinction: implementation can validate a spec, but it should not silently become the source of truth over it.\n\nThe CI failure you flagged is separately resolved: the replacement audit job passed after `c096075c`; the exception is limited to the non-shipping Moon test-host dependency chain."}],"phase":"final_answer"},"started_at_ms":1788301194224,"completed_at_ms":1788301197306}}
