{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:21:25.714Z","ordinal":7965,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a04adc-934d-7aa3-b352-bdba70d0f67c","turn_id":"01a05f0f-b170-7850-909e-14603cd5ff12","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_066dfde3c06b8029016a974fdbe97087d084014f53563d7379","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"Yes. The important design is to separate two dimensions that are currently being mixed:\n\n1. Is this document authoritative?\n2. Has its implementation been completed and proven?\n\nW3C and IETF both distinguish document maturity from implementation evidence. W3C’s useful pattern is: working draft → candidate recommendation (stable enough to gather implementation experience) → recommendation. Its current process explicitly treats the candidate stage as technically complete enough for implementation validation, not merely “some code was written.” [W3C Process](https://www.w3.org/policies/process/) The IETF similarly treats drafts as evolving review artifacts and requires demonstrated implementation/operational experience for mature standards. [RFC 2026](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2026.html)\n\nFor Callisto, I’d use this simpler model:\n\n| Specification status | Meaning | May authorize production implementation? |\n|---|---|---:|\n| `draft` | Active design; details can change freely. | No |\n| `in_review` | Proposed for formal architectural/process review. | No |\n| `accepted` | Approved, normative source of truth. Changes require a spec revision. | Yes |\n| `superseded` | Replaced by a named newer specification. | No |\n| `withdrawn` | Deliberately abandoned; retained for history. | No |\n\nThen add a separate implementation field:\n\n| Implementation status | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `not_started` | No implementation has begun. |\n| `in_progress` | Work is underway against the accepted spec. |\n| `implemented` | Required code/docs/workflows are present. |\n| `verified` | Traceability, tests, CI, and acceptance criteria have been independently checked. |\n\nThis avoids the misleading linear lifecycle `draft → accepted → implemented`, because acceptance and implementation are different facts. A spec can be accepted but not implemented; code can exist while a spec remains draft, but that should be flagged as a process exception—not silently treated as acceptance.\n\nFor 007–013 specifically, I would:\n\n- Keep them `draft` until the formal review actually occurs.\n- Perform a traceability review from each acceptance criterion to code/tests/workflows.\n- Record any deviations or clarifications in the spec itself.\n- Change them to `accepted` once that review approves their normative wording.\n- Set implementation to `verified` only after the independent validation Claude described.\n\nI would avoid importing W3C’s full “candidate recommendation” state unless you genuinely want to permit implementation before acceptance to gather evidence. Your stated process is clearer and safer: accepted specs authorize implementation; implementation evidence validates the accepted spec afterward."}],"phase":"final_answer"},"started_at_ms":1788301275960,"completed_at_ms":1788301285714}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:21:25.718Z","ordinal":7966,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_066dfde3c06b8029016a974fdbe97087d084014f53563d7379","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"Yes. The important design is to separate two dimensions that are currently being mixed:\n\n1. Is this document authoritative?\n2. Has its implementation been completed and proven?\n\nW3C and IETF both distinguish document maturity from implementation evidence. W3C’s useful pattern is: working draft → candidate recommendation (stable enough to gather implementation experience) → recommendation. Its current process explicitly treats the candidate stage as technically complete enough for implementation validation, not merely “some code was written.” [W3C Process](https://www.w3.org/policies/process/) The IETF similarly treats drafts as evolving review artifacts and requires demonstrated implementation/operational experience for mature standards. [RFC 2026](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2026.html)\n\nFor Callisto, I’d use this simpler model:\n\n| Specification status | Meaning | May authorize production implementation? |\n|---|---|---:|\n| `draft` | Active design; details can change freely. | No |\n| `in_review` | Proposed for formal architectural/process review. | No |\n| `accepted` | Approved, normative source of truth. Changes require a spec revision. | Yes |\n| `superseded` | Replaced by a named newer specification. | No |\n| `withdrawn` | Deliberately abandoned; retained for history. | No |\n\nThen add a separate implementation field:\n\n| Implementation status | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `not_started` | No implementation has begun. |\n| `in_progress` | Work is underway against the accepted spec. |\n| `implemented` | Required code/docs/workflows are present. |\n| `verified` | Traceability, tests, CI, and acceptance criteria have been independently checked. |\n\nThis avoids the misleading linear lifecycle `draft → accepted → implemented`, because acceptance and implementation are different facts. A spec can be accepted but not implemented; code can exist while a spec remains draft, but that should be flagged as a process exception—not silently treated as acceptance.\n\nFor 007–013 specifically, I would:\n\n- Keep them `draft` until the formal review actually occurs.\n- Perform a traceability review from each acceptance criterion to code/tests/workflows.\n- Record any deviations or clarifications in the spec itself.\n- Change them to `accepted` once that review approves their normative wording.\n- Set implementation to `verified` only after the independent validation Claude described.\n\nI would avoid importing W3C’s full “candidate recommendation” state unless you genuinely want to permit implementation before acceptance to gather evidence. Your stated process is clearer and safer: accepted specs authorize implementation; implementation evidence validates the accepted spec afterward."}],"phase":"final_answer","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a05f0f-b170-7850-909e-14603cd5ff12","create_time":1788301265.974135,"content_item_kinds":["unknown"]}}}
