{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:28:12.172Z","ordinal":8149,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a04adc-934d-7aa3-b352-bdba70d0f67c","turn_id":"01a05f10-4390-7220-8c20-684f83cf240e","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_066dfde3c06b8029016a97517a732887d08e3c12216e462806","summary_text":[],"raw_content":[]},"started_at_ms":1788301690463,"completed_at_ms":1788301692172}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:28:12.172Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"id":"rs_066dfde3c06b8029016a97517a732887d08e3c12216e462806","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a05f10-4390-7220-8c20-684f83cf240e"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:28:25.648Z","ordinal":8151,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a04adc-934d-7aa3-b352-bdba70d0f67c","turn_id":"01a05f10-4390-7220-8c20-684f83cf240e","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_066dfde3c06b8029016a97517c252887d09dd0c0ae200885ce","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"Yes. I’d add three layers of feedback, without giving untrusted PR code write permissions.\n\n1. GitHub job summary — built in, no action\n\nHave the test and coverage jobs write a short Markdown table to `$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`: total/passed/failed/skipped tests, total coverage, per-crate coverage, and links to artifacts. GitHub renders this directly on the workflow run. [GitHub job summaries](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/writing-workflows/choosing-what-your-workflow-does/workflow-commands-for-github-actions?tool=powershell)\n\nI’ve already pushed `18a3514`, which makes the coverage job print its percentage before applying the 90% gate and upload `lcov.info` even when the gate fails. That makes the immediate failure diagnosable.\n\n2. Downloadable coverage reports\n\nKeep LCOV for tools, and generate/upload:\n\n- `lcov.info` — interoperable raw report.\n- Cobertura XML — broadly consumable.\n- HTML report — easiest for a reviewer to inspect locally.\n\n`cargo-llvm-cov` supports generating alternate reports from the same completed test run, so this does not rerun tests. [cargo-llvm-cov reporting](https://github.com/taiki-e/cargo-llvm-cov/blob/main/README.md)\n\n3. Sticky PR report\n\nFor coverage, `zgosalvez/lcov-reporter-action` can create/update one PR comment, report changed-line coverage, attach HTML, and enforce a threshold. I would use it for reporting only; `just coverage 90` remains the authoritative gate. [Report LCOV action](https://github.com/marketplace/actions/report-lcov)\n\nFor test results, configure Nextest’s CI profile to emit JUnit XML, then publish it using `EnricoMi/publish-unit-test-result-action`. It creates a check summary, annotations for failures, a job summary, and updates one PR comment rather than spamming comments. [Nextest JUnit support](https://www.nexte.st/docs/machine-readable/junit/), [test-results action](https://github.com/marketplace/actions/publish-test-results)\n\nSecurity matters here: don’t grant `pull-requests: write` to the existing `pull_request` CI workflow. Fork PRs receive read-only tokens, and using `pull_request_target` with checked-out PR code is dangerous. [GitHub permissions](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-syntax), [secure workflow guidance](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/secure-use?learn=getting_started&learnProduct=actions)\n\nInstead:\n\n```text\nuntrusted PR CI\n  → generates JUnit / LCOV / summary artifacts\n  → uploads artifacts\n\ntrusted workflow_run reporter\n  → downloads only those artifacts\n  → never checks out PR code\n  → has narrowly scoped pull-requests: write\n  → updates one sticky PR comment\n```\n\nGitHub explicitly recommends that privilege separation approach, with caution around artifact content. [workflow_run guidance](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/events-that-trigger-workflows?ref=content-for-engineers-by-engineers-atomist-blog)\n\nMy recommendation: implement the built-in summaries and artifacts first, then add the separate trusted reporter workflow with SHA-pinned reporting actions."}],"phase":"final_answer"},"started_at_ms":1788301692173,"completed_at_ms":1788301705648}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:28:25.656Z","ordinal":8152,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_066dfde3c06b8029016a97517c252887d09dd0c0ae200885ce","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"Yes. I’d add three layers of feedback, without giving untrusted PR code write permissions.\n\n1. GitHub job summary — built in, no action\n\nHave the test and coverage jobs write a short Markdown table to `$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`: total/passed/failed/skipped tests, total coverage, per-crate coverage, and links to artifacts. GitHub renders this directly on the workflow run. [GitHub job summaries](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/writing-workflows/choosing-what-your-workflow-does/workflow-commands-for-github-actions?tool=powershell)\n\nI’ve already pushed `18a3514`, which makes the coverage job print its percentage before applying the 90% gate and upload `lcov.info` even when the gate fails. That makes the immediate failure diagnosable.\n\n2. Downloadable coverage reports\n\nKeep LCOV for tools, and generate/upload:\n\n- `lcov.info` — interoperable raw report.\n- Cobertura XML — broadly consumable.\n- HTML report — easiest for a reviewer to inspect locally.\n\n`cargo-llvm-cov` supports generating alternate reports from the same completed test run, so this does not rerun tests. [cargo-llvm-cov reporting](https://github.com/taiki-e/cargo-llvm-cov/blob/main/README.md)\n\n3. Sticky PR report\n\nFor coverage, `zgosalvez/lcov-reporter-action` can create/update one PR comment, report changed-line coverage, attach HTML, and enforce a threshold. I would use it for reporting only; `just coverage 90` remains the authoritative gate. [Report LCOV action](https://github.com/marketplace/actions/report-lcov)\n\nFor test results, configure Nextest’s CI profile to emit JUnit XML, then publish it using `EnricoMi/publish-unit-test-result-action`. It creates a check summary, annotations for failures, a job summary, and updates one PR comment rather than spamming comments. [Nextest JUnit support](https://www.nexte.st/docs/machine-readable/junit/), [test-results action](https://github.com/marketplace/actions/publish-test-results)\n\nSecurity matters here: don’t grant `pull-requests: write` to the existing `pull_request` CI workflow. Fork PRs receive read-only tokens, and using `pull_request_target` with checked-out PR code is dangerous. [GitHub permissions](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-syntax), [secure workflow guidance](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/secure-use?learn=getting_started&learnProduct=actions)\n\nInstead:\n\n```text\nuntrusted PR CI\n  → generates JUnit / LCOV / summary artifacts\n  → uploads artifacts\n\ntrusted workflow_run reporter\n  → downloads only those artifacts\n  → never checks out PR code\n  → has narrowly scoped pull-requests: write\n  → updates one sticky PR comment\n```\n\nGitHub explicitly recommends that privilege separation approach, with caution around artifact content. [workflow_run guidance](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/events-that-trigger-workflows?ref=content-for-engineers-by-engineers-atomist-blog)\n\nMy recommendation: implement the built-in summaries and artifacts first, then add the separate trusted reporter workflow with SHA-pinned reporting actions."}],"phase":"final_answer","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a05f10-4390-7220-8c20-684f83cf240e","create_time":1788301689.875555,"content_item_kinds":["unknown"]}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:28:25.684Z","ordinal":8153,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"token_count","info":{"total_token_usage":{"input_tokens":141817346,"cached_input_tokens":136857344,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":392304,"reasoning_output_tokens":126664,"total_tokens":142209650},"last_token_usage":{"input_tokens":137547,"cached_input_tokens":130816,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":790,"reasoning_output_tokens":93,"total_tokens":138337},"model_context_window":258400},"rate_limits":{"limit_id":"codex","limit_name":null,"primary":{"used_percent":15.0,"window_minutes":10080,"resets_at":1788799514},"secondary":null,"credits":{"has_credits":false,"unlimited":false,"balance":"0"},"individual_limit":null,"spend_control_reached":null,"plan_type":"prolite","rate_limit_reached_type":null}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:28:25.691Z","ordinal":8154,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"task_complete","turn_id":"01a05f10-4390-7220-8c20-684f83cf240e","last_agent_message":"Yes. I’d add three layers of feedback, without giving untrusted PR code write permissions.\n\n1. GitHub job summary — built in, no action\n\nHave the test and coverage jobs write a short Markdown table to `$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`: total/passed/failed/skipped tests, total coverage, per-crate coverage, and links to artifacts. GitHub renders this directly on the workflow run. [GitHub job summaries](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/writing-workflows/choosing-what-your-workflow-does/workflow-commands-for-github-actions?tool=powershell)\n\nI’ve already pushed `18a3514`, which makes the coverage job print its percentage before applying the 90% gate and upload `lcov.info` even when the gate fails. That makes the immediate failure diagnosable.\n\n2. Downloadable coverage reports\n\nKeep LCOV for tools, and generate/upload:\n\n- `lcov.info` — interoperable raw report.\n- Cobertura XML — broadly consumable.\n- HTML report — easiest for a reviewer to inspect locally.\n\n`cargo-llvm-cov` supports generating alternate reports from the same completed test run, so this does not rerun tests. [cargo-llvm-cov reporting](https://github.com/taiki-e/cargo-llvm-cov/blob/main/README.md)\n\n3. Sticky PR report\n\nFor coverage, `zgosalvez/lcov-reporter-action` can create/update one PR comment, report changed-line coverage, attach HTML, and enforce a threshold. I would use it for reporting only; `just coverage 90` remains the authoritative gate. [Report LCOV action](https://github.com/marketplace/actions/report-lcov)\n\nFor test results, configure Nextest’s CI profile to emit JUnit XML, then publish it using `EnricoMi/publish-unit-test-result-action`. It creates a check summary, annotations for failures, a job summary, and updates one PR comment rather than spamming comments. [Nextest JUnit support](https://www.nexte.st/docs/machine-readable/junit/), [test-results action](https://github.com/marketplace/actions/publish-test-results)\n\nSecurity matters here: don’t grant `pull-requests: write` to the existing `pull_request` CI workflow. Fork PRs receive read-only tokens, and using `pull_request_target` with checked-out PR code is dangerous. [GitHub permissions](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-syntax), [secure workflow guidance](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/secure-use?learn=getting_started&learnProduct=actions)\n\nInstead:\n\n```text\nuntrusted PR CI\n  → generates JUnit / LCOV / summary artifacts\n  → uploads artifacts\n\ntrusted workflow_run reporter\n  → downloads only those artifacts\n  → never checks out PR code\n  → has narrowly scoped pull-requests: write\n  → updates one sticky PR comment\n```\n\nGitHub explicitly recommends that privilege separation approach, with caution around artifact content. [workflow_run guidance](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/events-that-trigger-workflows?ref=content-for-engineers-by-engineers-atomist-blog)\n\nMy recommendation: implement the built-in summaries and artifacts first, then add the separate trusted reporter workflow with SHA-pinned reporting actions.","started_at":1788301296,"completed_at":1788301705,"duration_ms":409147,"time_to_first_token_ms":3365}}
