{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:35:59.695Z","ordinal":8286,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a04adc-934d-7aa3-b352-bdba70d0f67c","turn_id":"01a05f18-d73e-7dd3-85d2-d3a848bd8fd1","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_066dfde3c06b8029016a97534e307487d0970feccbfc38b893","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"Yes—exactly. The release foundation needs spec-driven black-box tests, not unit tests written to chase red coverage lines.\n\nThe data confirms it: `main` is 91.49% line coverage; PR #39 adds roughly 3,878 executable lines at only about 71.9%. The meaningful fix is an end-to-end release test matrix through the CLI and real temporary Git repositories, with controlled fake registry/forge tools. I’ll reorient the test work around those observable contracts; small model-level tests remain useful for canonicalization and state-machine invariants, but they are not the primary proof."}],"phase":"commentary"},"started_at_ms":1788302158222,"completed_at_ms":1788302159695}}
