{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:37:00.351Z","ordinal":8318,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a04adc-934d-7aa3-b352-bdba70d0f67c","turn_id":"01a05f18-d73e-7dd3-85d2-d3a848bd8fd1","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_066dfde3c06b8029016a97538673ec87d09c771b1fd3aa9857","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"Yes. Black-box, spec-driven tests should be the primary proof:\n\n- Drive the real `callisto` binary against temporary Git repositories.\n- Exercise complete success flows, interruption/retry behavior, invalid inputs, stale commit/intent rejection, credential-bearing registry rejection, and no-unintended-side-effects guarantees.\n- Use controlled fake `cargo`, `npm`, and `gh` executables only at external boundaries; assert observable Git state, artifacts, command order, and JSON output.\n- Keep focused model tests for things that black-box tests cannot isolate well—canonical digests, parsing, and state-machine invariants. They complement, rather than replace, behavioral tests.\n\nThe evidence makes this important: `main` is at 91.49% line coverage; PR #39 is 89.12%. The new release code is materially under-exercised, so lowering the gate would hide a real gap.\n\nI also implemented and pushed the reporting foundation in `d1dbc55`:\n\n- Nextest produces JUnit in CI.\n- CI uploads JUnit and LCOV artifacts even when a gate fails.\n- A SHA-pinned, trusted `workflow_run` reporter publishes two sticky PR comments: Nextest results and LCOV coverage.\n- The normal PR CI now has only `contents: read`; only the reporter gets `pull-requests: write`, and it never checks out PR code.\n\nOne GitHub limitation: the new `workflow_run` reporter becomes active only after it exists on the default branch, so PR #39 itself will not receive those sticky comments until that workflow is merged."}],"phase":"final_answer"},"started_at_ms":1788302214480,"completed_at_ms":1788302220351}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:37:00.355Z","ordinal":8319,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_066dfde3c06b8029016a97538673ec87d09c771b1fd3aa9857","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"Yes. Black-box, spec-driven tests should be the primary proof:\n\n- Drive the real `callisto` binary against temporary Git repositories.\n- Exercise complete success flows, interruption/retry behavior, invalid inputs, stale commit/intent rejection, credential-bearing registry rejection, and no-unintended-side-effects guarantees.\n- Use controlled fake `cargo`, `npm`, and `gh` executables only at external boundaries; assert observable Git state, artifacts, command order, and JSON output.\n- Keep focused model tests for things that black-box tests cannot isolate well—canonical digests, parsing, and state-machine invariants. They complement, rather than replace, behavioral tests.\n\nThe evidence makes this important: `main` is at 91.49% line coverage; PR #39 is 89.12%. The new release code is materially under-exercised, so lowering the gate would hide a real gap.\n\nI also implemented and pushed the reporting foundation in `d1dbc55`:\n\n- Nextest produces JUnit in CI.\n- CI uploads JUnit and LCOV artifacts even when a gate fails.\n- A SHA-pinned, trusted `workflow_run` reporter publishes two sticky PR comments: Nextest results and LCOV coverage.\n- The normal PR CI now has only `contents: read`; only the reporter gets `pull-requests: write`, and it never checks out PR code.\n\nOne GitHub limitation: the new `workflow_run` reporter becomes active only after it exists on the default branch, so PR #39 itself will not receive those sticky comments until that workflow is merged."}],"phase":"final_answer","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a05f18-d73e-7dd3-85d2-d3a848bd8fd1","create_time":1788302204.074851,"content_item_kinds":["unknown"]}}}
