{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:44:07.355Z","type":"compacted","payload":{"first_window_id":"01a04adc-934d-7aa3-b352-bdcab9ffb881","message":"","previous_window_id":"01a05ee7-ed67-7de1-872d-55ace7376c39","replacement_history":[{"content":[{"text":"Please take a look at this repo, understand the current claude setup and its plugins as well as the codebase. I need your help in optimizing the setup for GPT also. What do you think","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04ade-4aeb-77e0-b2dd-3404dc3e1c9b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1787962477.291803,"turn_id":"01a04ade-4981-70d0-ac56-530c7b97d294"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Please take a look at this repo, understand the current claude setup and its plugins as well as the codebase. I need your help in optimizing the setup for GPT also. What do you think","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04ade-6208-7d21-a807-fe380df3658e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1787962483.20854,"turn_id":"01a04ade-61f8-7b53-9087-d8632d46b38c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Yes, do that","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04ae2-ef20-7e01-82c2-470bcfcbc2b7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1787962781.472175,"turn_id":"01a04ae2-ef01-7dc1-a5ac-3739a61781ba"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"What's next? Can you read the docs, specs, plans, etc?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04e29-da12-7830-83f5-cebdaed61164","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788017760.786165,"turn_id":"01a04e29-d84b-7063-b888-0a35c9f4a994"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Please read all of those to get a deep understanding of our codebase and further suggest setup changes. Also do research to understand our competition including changesets, knope, release please and nx release. Review our docs and specs to see if we missed anything. Consider arch, semantic models, DX, performance and security.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04e2e-4716-7861-9620-8d6a24be8e37","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788018050.838903,"turn_id":"01a04e2e-4553-7fe0-a960-d0aacc670509"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Shouldn't we adjust the spec as we learn? They are our source of truth and future AI reading them or folks reading them will be confused. Git preserves the history already anyway.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04e47-e2a6-7e72-8e8a-0bb51c7cb022","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788019729.062564,"turn_id":"01a04e47-e12c-77b1-8ffa-47c6716238af"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"GIve me more details on the 2-4 above, durable plans, SHA, etc. I want to understand what you're proposing","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04e53-c306-7bf0-adbd-24ecdb02555c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788020507.398858,"turn_id":"01a04e53-c14c-7d23-b07b-78ce00854834"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"When the plan is running in CI how do we best handle this? Is there a problem to checking in files that we can forget to clean up? Should it be cached by GH instead?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04e57-029d-7c40-af13-70fc5e402558","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788020720.285472,"turn_id":"01a04e57-027e-7280-9358-bc608f32850c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"How do we setup the SHA/signing with GH? What if folks use something other than GH? SOmething like Bezel? Etc","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04e58-af73-71e1-bcce-efbf699c7ae7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788020830.067689,"turn_id":"01a04e58-adfa-7d20-9fcc-d0a0ea3e13cd"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"What is the security posture, issues, etc?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04e5a-00ef-71a3-b204-2f712987bf99","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788020916.462999,"turn_id":"01a04e5a-00d6-7fc3-951c-65e9b3df71d5"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Can we write up specs for all of these thinsg you're suggesting? Create a branch in a separate worktree to avoid clashing with claude. Check the current stacked status of PRs and create a PR for the specs?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04e5b-fd23-7cb2-9e63-4743f0a5c5b2","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788021046.563358,"turn_id":"01a04e5b-fb33-79d1-b7a6-2883a9f09aaa"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Can you now do an adversarial audit of our codebase looking for bugs, errors, DX issues, performance issues or security bugs? Report back","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04e63-6f86-77a1-8476-200c6d8b8e09","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788021534.598498,"turn_id":"01a04e63-6f49-7d00-b1a4-ff5ba15d830b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Can you validate this against the open PRs? What is the plan to address them? Think comprehensively, architectually and semantically about the issue in relation to our architecture and models. Are there crates we should leverage that we're not?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04e73-93fc-7260-bee4-e206b42ac175","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788022592.508199,"turn_id":"01a04e73-937f-7ae0-80b7-2961f6079474"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"FYI ci is failing: https://github.com/orin-dx/callisto/pull/32","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04e75-89e8-7e02-a844-901ef7628cf5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788022721.000105,"turn_id":"01a04e73-937f-7ae0-80b7-2961f6079474"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"feedback from claude:\n\nCross-cutting issues (apply to all six)\n\n1. No requirement@1 exists for any of them. No .claude/requirements/ directory exists at all — every spec cites a linked_requirement (REQ-*) with no backing file. Repo-wide process gap, not a defect in any one spec. Needs a decision: backfill requirements, or drop linked_requirement and treat specs as top-level.\n2. A dependency chain is implicit, not stated. GITHUB-HARDENING-003 AC-004 and SOURCE-PROVENANCE-002 both assume PLAN-DURABILITY-001's artifact-handoff/job-split exists. None of the three specs declares this dependency explicitly. Build order must be 001 → (002, 003) if attempted at all.\n3. PLAN-DURABILITY-001 assumes a two-job topology that doesn't exist. Today plan-publish→publish→filter-plan→tag all run in one release job, one shell block, in callisto-action. The artifact-upload/download AC presupposes a job split nothing in the spec calls out as an architecture change.\n\nPer-spec verdicts\n\n001 — Plan Durability: Biggest open risk is the \"normalized configuration digest\" — no algorithm specified, and PublishPlan's serde skip_serializing_if optional fields make naive JSON hashing unstable. Two failure directions are asymmetric: too-strict fails safe, too-loose executes a stale/wrong plan against real registry credentials. Secret-scanning AC is ambiguous (structural guarantee vs. heuristic regex scan — very different implementations). Needs the digest algorithm and secret-check mechanism pinned down before planning.\n\n002 — Source Provenance: Low implementation risk todayHEAD is stable throughout — the equality check isnearly tautological in the current architecture) but becomes load-bearing the moment 001's job-split ships (a race window opens betweeplan-job and execute-job checkouts). Spec-quality gap:llides with existing ReleaseEntry.sha (plan.rs:130)with no criterion forcing them equal. AC-003 mostly re-specifies already-shipped CreatedTag.already_existed — only the \"annotated tag\"part is actually new. AC-004/005 (signing policy) are no data shape, no named consumer.\n                                                                                                                                      003 — GitHub Release Hardening: Real, well-scoped gapsual workflow — no top-level permissions:, no SHA-pinned actions, release job over-grants write scopes even for changeset-PR-only runs. Composite-action architecture means the split doesn't  affect external callisto-action consumers (low couplinrisk: a GitHub Environment gate is invisible in theworkflow diff — the actual protection-rule config lives outside the reviewed file, so \"references environment:\" isn't provable safety without an out-of-band check. AC-001 doesn't state the (same gap noted in cross-cutting #3). AC-005 is likely redundant with AC-001 and should either be cut or rewritten around a concrete mechanism (e.g. CODEOWNERS on .github/workflows/).\n\n004 — Supply Chain Reproducibility: Verified premise is real and currently worse than the spec assumes — the yanked bisync advisory    reaches production code (callisto-cli/callisto-graph),ike the 11 already-ignored advisories. AC-001 (\"records the advisory database revision\") reads as a native cargo-deny flag; it isn't — recording the db revision requires a workaround         (separate git rev-parse HEAD step against the live adv\"bounded exception\" is never defined (no max expirystated). AC-004 (secrets out of logs) is untestable — no secrets currently touch this pipeline at all.                                 \n005 — Documentation Authority: All three \"stale doc\" claims independently verified true (design.md's status header contradicts its own milestones section; version-flow.md describes a bug ineady fixed; error-taxonomy.md's stale claim was already confirmed earlier). AC-001 and AC-004 are the weak points — neither specifies a concrete location/mechanism (where does the authority  index live? what pattern detects a \"current behavior ae by both a rigorous and a trivial-compliance solutionequally. AC-004 also risks flagging its own protected historical/plan documents (non_goals conflict).                                  \n006 — Lane Policy: (from earlier report) AC-002's premise is factually false against already-shipped groups.rs conflict-detection code — the spec asks for something that already exists, neeing.\n\nRecommendation\n\nNone of the six is implementation-ready as written. Ifrything else leans on its artifact/digest design), pinthe digest algorithm and job-split scope explicitly, then 002/003 in parallel, 004/005/006 are independent and can proceed once their spec-quality gaps (missing mechanisms, false premises,ed by canon:drafter revision passes.\n\nPer your standing instruction, this stays research onlted on any of the six.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04ec9-387d-7112-aaa4-61deb0ab7a63","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788028205.181404,"turn_id":"01a04ec9-37e6-7e01-99fa-7e15fa1be18b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Claude is implementing the specs you worked on. Not finished but can you take a look at whats ongoing and evaluate it?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a04fab-1156-7980-9c2a-6d6ae9e99dba","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788043006.294482,"turn_id":"01a04fab-0f6a-7532-a7df-1f67fe2ad740"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"We've split some of the specs you had scoped out and implemented most of it. Can you please analyze the work we did after re-reading through the specs to consider what we implemented?? Do a very thorough analysis looking for compliance, DX, security, performance and actually achieving our goals.\n\nAlso consider where claude is stuck:\n\nThis round's results are two different stories.\n\n005 is nearly done — this is real, close signal. All three previously-flagged verify gaps are fully resolved. The lambda:exit-gate agent even ran mutation testing by hand (deliberately breaking the code 8 different ways and confirming the tests catch it) and found exactly one missing test: merging the docs/.md and semantic-model/.md valid-authority-value sets together wouldn't be caught by any existing assertion, even though that's the exact cross-glob-contamination bug AC-004 exists to prevent. That's a single, well-defined test to add — this one is essentially done.\n\n001A, 001B, 001C, 004, and 006 all failed again, but the character of the failures has changed. These aren't spec-wording gaps anymore — they're real architecture questions:\n\n- 001A: digest computation needs data from callisto-graph (config resolution, package discovery), but the spec places it in callisto-model, which callisto-graph depends on — so as specified, this would require a dependency cycle. That's not a wording fix, that's a real \"where does this code actually live\" decision. Also found a genuine cross-build correctness bug: DepKind's derived Ord (declaration order) disagrees with its serialized string's lexicographic order, so the \"sorted by kind\" comparator is ambiguous and two conforming builds would produce different digests for the same input — silently defeating the entire point of the digest scheme.\n- 001B: found a real logic bug — under the new status record, publish.rs's existing success check (!is_failure()) would treat every never-attempted operation as \"shipped\" after an interruption, meaning callisto tag could tag and release packages that were never actually published.\n- 001C, 004, 006: narrower but still substantive — an undefined CLI argument, a contradiction between two criteria's rules for the empty-plan case, an unenforceable \"prove this function was never called\" test requirement, and (006) a genuinely unsatisfiable \"reject the package\" rule once more than one package qualifies.\n\nThis is a different kind of blocker than what we've been fixing all session — these need someone to make actual design calls (where does digest logic live, what's the real fix for the tag-after-interrupted-publish bug), not another verify-and-patch round. Given how long this has run, I'd suggest: land 005 now (one test, trivial), and treat 001A/001B/001C/004/006 as a separate, focused design pass — probably worth a fresh session with your direct involvement on the architecture calls rather than more autonomous iteration here.\n\nReport back first. Don't rush to fix or change things. Analyze, re-evaluate and consider what we have and what we need to address.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0504c-7335-7cb2-93a2-f33dee4b70f4","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788053582.645924,"turn_id":"01a0504c-71b5-79a2-a307-afba1ec59657"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Did you consider our architecture? Our semantic models? I don't want you to rush to solutions. I want you to consider the issues in the context of the entire system and propose comprehensive solutions that are in keeping with the rest of the system. Consider it all holistically. Research if necessary, consider crates to use or better using what we have, adjusting our architecture and specs.\n\nDon't rush on this. It's important to get the architecture and plan correctly first.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05050-d969-7b60-8f2b-eb0ce7874de2","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788053870.953444,"turn_id":"01a05050-d951-7011-9f40-70e1d099ee72"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Do you understand how we implement our work from specs and validate/verify using our claude plugins and agents? If you are to attempt to actually do the work you need to follow our process well.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05054-1a2a-7ab2-a9b8-c075325ee2ca","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788054084.138267,"turn_id":"01a05050-d951-7011-9f40-70e1d099ee72"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Don't forget to consider RS best practices, language features in your proposal. We can consider changes, traits, etc. Do we leverage rayon, miette, etc?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05054-1a2c-7ef3-baff-08efc09362c1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788054084.140109,"turn_id":"01a05050-d951-7011-9f40-70e1d099ee72"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Once you've considered all of this present it to me please. I'll share with claude for a second opinion","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05054-1a2d-7cb3-b89f-5c3aa33d0397","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788054084.141819,"turn_id":"01a05050-d951-7011-9f40-70e1d099ee72"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Response from claude:\n\nVerified the load-bearing citations against real code — all check out exactly as claimed: ApplyPermit's capability-token pattern (permit.rs), PackageId::matches' deliberate bare-name wildcard behavior with its own caller-contract warning (identity.rs:122-129), PublishAttemptResult::is_failure/PublishReport::has_failures matching only Failed (report.rs:94-105), and Workspace's RefCell/OnceCell fields (lib.rs:5,53). Nothing fabricated.\n\nSubstantively: this is stronger than where I'd gotten to, and I'd defer to it. Specific points where it's genuinely better, not just different:\n\n- The ValidatedReleaseIntent opaque-capability design resolves the crate-cycle problem more cleanly than anything I proposed. I flagged the cycle but left \"where does digest logic live\" as an open question; Codex's answer — callisto-graph builds an opaque, non-Serialize, non-forgeable type, and mutation requires holding both it and \u0026ApplyPermit — is a real solution, not a workaround, and it's the same shape as the existing ApplyPermit pattern rather than a new one bolted on.\n- PackageId::matches being wrong for release-operation identity is a genuinely new finding. None of my reconciliation or gate rounds caught this. It's correct: that method is explicitly documented as an ecosystem-wildcard matcher for human-facing lookups, and using it for exact operation identity would silently conflate packages across ecosystems that share a bare name.\n- The receipt-model framing is more principled than my \"needs a decision\" note on the tag-after-interrupted-publish bug. \"Every operation has exactly one terminal outcome, absence of failure cannot mean success\" is the actual fix, not \"add more enum variants and hope callers update.\"\n- The registry-URL-secret point is real and new. CratePublish.registry: Option\u003cString\u003e — confirmed, raw string, could carry userinfo or a token-bearing query string on a custom index. Nothing in this session's audit rounds touched that.\n\nTwo things worth adding to their write-up before it becomes the accepted spec:\n\n1. The olpc-cjson vs. JCS/RFC 8785 tradeoff should be an explicit, argued decision in the spec, not just resolved in Codex's favor by default. Their point is correct — olpc-cjson's own docs say it can emit non-standard JSON — but that only matters if something outside this Rust codebase needs to independently recompute the digest. If verification only ever happens in-process (Rust reading Rust-written bytes), strict RFC 8785 compliance is a nice-to-have, not load-bearing, and serde_json_canonicalizer is a less production-proven dependency than olpc-cjson (already shipping in tough, already in Cargo.lock). Worth having trace settle this against whether cross-language verification is an actual requirement rather than a hypothetical one.\n2. Connect 006's open decision explicitly to the bug fix-006-final already found. Codex's \"decide whether prerelease is workspace-global shaping or explicit release-set authority\" is exactly the bump_target-has-no-roster-check finding from this session (an off-roster package still gets prerelease-tagged in PreMode::Pre because the check that would gate it doesn't exist). Worth citing that concretely in the foundational spec rather than re-discovering it.\n\nEverything else — the Rayon/Tokio rejection, the miette-vs-durable-JSON separation, the #[non_exhaustive] guidance, deferring all six specs until the foundational contract lands — I have no pushback on. Go with it.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0505c-2e28-7322-9d06-67df6d310013","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788054613.544968,"turn_id":"01a0505c-2cef-7f91-9391-d3663772ed62"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's take a stab at implementing this since you scoped it out better than Claude did. Giving you a change to show us that you can do better than the previous AIs attemtping this. Show me that you're capable of following our process appropriately and delivering.\n\nClaude commited it's work so now do yours.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0505f-5d49-7482-af27-0c3f18bca037","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788054822.217368,"turn_id":"01a0505f-5bcc-76b0-bec7-39d9b220aa49"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Don't rely on claude CLI, use your own agent and subagent ability to do this yourself","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0507a-3b0b-78d2-9f77-61359c9209e0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788056582.923604,"turn_id":"01a0507a-3a96-70b3-9859-fb1f0836f6c6"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Keep it going until one","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0507f-f3fa-7ad0-85cc-ddbea718b510","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788056957.946091,"turn_id":"01a0507f-f294-7860-b292-371ed6d4bbf5"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Do it and then start the code please","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05083-57cf-7660-ac4b-97ed84efe945","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788057180.111322,"turn_id":"01a05083-57b4-7b73-b487-5b012e1f07b9"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Do it","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a058b5-cec7-7e21-bd08-df477b3563c2","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788194705.095354,"turn_id":"01a058b5-ccd6-7260-8b11-bd8028715579"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"What's next?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a058db-1ece-7b71-a80e-361ebe65056e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788197150.414336,"turn_id":"01a058db-1cec-75b3-9cbf-e7720d481075"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Sounds good, go ahead","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a058e1-68bd-7543-a960-e2918af44909","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788197562.557867,"turn_id":"01a058e1-6764-7de3-ba6a-9cccd0480998"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Go ahead and get it done then","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a058ea-4ecc-7fc1-b907-2e583df11304","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788198145.740076,"turn_id":"01a058ea-4d4b-7b30-a1f2-e3b4e1ed98f1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Go ahead and get it done then. Use agents and workflows (agent teams in claude, not sure what you call it, swarm?) to get the work done without interfearing with each other","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a058ea-e830-7142-b5fe-120400b69e80","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788198185.008542,"turn_id":"01a058ea-e811-7141-83ec-7d7f6d7f2863"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"What's next?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a058fa-42cb-7382-838e-a964bd54ca2a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788199191.243236,"turn_id":"01a058fa-41a2-7563-822a-62d857f89d92"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Where are we in this? All done?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0591d-391c-7961-bbd9-9eaf9a3e4d6b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788201482.524151,"turn_id":"01a0591d-3738-7351-8c13-9e7bcb9542e2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's tak on all of the work we need to do please","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0591d-8fe9-7540-be0c-85ac8f76cec3","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788201504.745293,"turn_id":"01a0591d-8fce-7d61-9626-c01ee86681ee"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"All done?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05923-ac18-7a01-97a7-1e2b4321ca5a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788201905.176341,"turn_id":"01a05923-aa5b-7540-8bc8-e96c720d9c41"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Commite completed work in logical and semantic intervals and continue working unitl done","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05924-1e16-7e62-8139-0bd5892acbba","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788201934.358902,"turn_id":"01a05924-1d10-7df2-8fb7-f53675ffb394"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"It's hard ot tell  what you have running","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05930-b561-73a3-b28a-fa8152808335","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788202759.521204,"turn_id":"01a05930-b43d-7230-92a9-f3adf2afca3e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's keep working on it if nothing is running","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05931-40d9-7560-ac72-b24f2c8481c2","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788202795.225828,"turn_id":"01a05931-40bd-7991-b9c5-3c5f0b34b3a7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Everything done?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05952-b29e-7773-924b-ea2aab9fb1fb","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788204987.038693,"turn_id":"01a05952-b110-7af3-95ba-d26855d735d9"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"All done?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05992-83ae-7ff0-8a90-ada7005a35b7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788209169.326756,"turn_id":"01a05992-80ab-7092-89fe-9bc8e5ea55c4"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's finish everything please","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05995-1c9e-7881-96a2-9b633eb88982","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788209339.550976,"turn_id":"01a05995-1c80-7b61-a5e9-92f099e515e1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's do the review and correct the P0s then","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a059af-4873-7af3-b59e-84c3cdde7d3c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788211054.707336,"turn_id":"01a059af-46cf-74f1-b88b-b360db3f7fdc"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Now that you've found a bunch of things do we have to reevaluate or reconsider anythning in our models and architecture to better implement our features? Don't hold back in analyzing and considering how to do this properly. Report back","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a059b9-93d6-72b3-9c0d-4e94e7cc9b52","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788211729.36638,"turn_id":"01a059b9-92b1-7533-aa3c-77514c6bfa8e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Can we spec this out and consider a plan? Use our workflow and processes as you've learned from the claude plugins.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a059bd-4bf2-7da3-96b8-f123ab2e861d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788211973.106409,"turn_id":"01a059bd-495a-7851-b4b9-6bfdd9a82015"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Use plain english, this is a bunch of mumbo jumbo and I can't tell what you're trying to ask.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a059cd-144e-7a92-8a1e-1796f1e2dcdd","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788213007.438606,"turn_id":"01a059cd-130f-7153-b987-683fb5d22912"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"1. It depends on the configuration. If the packages are linked it should affect all. Otherwise only those included.\n\n2. Why is this an issue?\n\n3. How does our workflow work then?\n\n4. What's the best option here? We want to have things work out of the box but be customizable if need be","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a059cf-4194-78c1-bd6e-a7935a401d4a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788213150.100305,"turn_id":"01a059cf-417a-7921-88ed-9a4ee472a5f2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"2. your git-only sounds good\n3. That seems fine as long as we're considering some of our packages are compiled to binary before publish (say rust publishing to crate as source and GH as binary)\n4. sound good","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a059d2-1009-7663-8d51-eb8020968b71","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788213334.025766,"turn_id":"01a059d2-0ee3-7140-9702-7197abead5ef"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Update the spec, yes","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a059d4-36fe-7782-bf37-1d2521ccebf8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788213475.070959,"turn_id":"01a059d4-35ea-7331-9ee3-58be5f101528"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's implement everything and fix all of the issues please","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a059e4-0bcf-7552-81c9-43af90427f2b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788214512.591685,"turn_id":"01a059e4-0a67-7f22-8e41-6c18783cbd6b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's go ahead","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a059ec-79b7-7921-9695-7af0dd85ca88","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788215065.014999,"turn_id":"01a059ec-784b-74f0-a64a-95998145bd1c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"yes please, we should include attestations","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a059f4-933d-7651-abf5-a6d3e7b293f4","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788215595.837587,"turn_id":"01a059f4-9211-7273-964e-6e070ed72984"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"build it all please","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05a0b-d677-7db1-854b-a9020f81760a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788217120.37574,"turn_id":"01a05a0b-d4d6-7540-a2d0-aa3417edb134"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Continue implementing","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05a53-8c2e-7093-8fd9-fbddf47d175b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788221819.950149,"turn_id":"01a05a53-8a5c-7622-a0fc-bf0e49c83e34"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Do the next now","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05a92-5c1c-7ca3-b05f-36794dd68caf","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788225936.412611,"turn_id":"01a05a92-59d2-7120-9e4e-0325932c991a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Follow through the next couple without stopping please","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05a98-1445-7950-b765-9bd6ce6232c6","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788226311.237847,"turn_id":"01a05a98-132c-7c02-870a-545ce904a002"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Go through all of it until complete like I keep asking","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05aa2-3375-75c3-a1a9-ca1391898948","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788226974.581125,"turn_id":"01a05aa2-31ed-73d2-b2cc-68f8d8cb7104"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Completed everything like I asked you to?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05b05-a04d-7382-893b-a7ca87a24702","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788233490.509974,"turn_id":"01a05b05-9e8e-7e63-90e7-5b0d393863d1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Continue until done please","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05b05-ed5c-7281-afb3-ba17d236dde5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788233510.236992,"turn_id":"01a05b05-ed43-7061-80ee-00e4099e370d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Continue work until done","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05b0c-d011-75a2-9f5b-5ea23bb93142","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788233961.489818,"turn_id":"01a05b0c-cfeb-75c1-a918-e152b53753f8"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Finish the work","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05b11-910c-7c31-887a-46877ca35cc6","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788234273.036606,"turn_id":"01a05b11-8f9b-7ff1-bdcf-583f2039c3e8"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Finish the work","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05b14-118b-7762-b4aa-4746c566a3aa","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788234437.003095,"turn_id":"01a05b14-105a-7e20-9812-dfcede90ca3c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Finish the work","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05b22-385e-7de3-a4d0-72aa14ff653e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788235364.446092,"turn_id":"01a05b22-36b9-7d01-99cf-2ddab702e0e2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's do that completely now. Don't stop until done","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05d49-20ee-7b13-bbe4-80a894d4ad52","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788271468.78294,"turn_id":"01a05d49-1eae-7852-a6cd-3d52b20e50f1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's implement what is left then. That's what I said, did you not understand me?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05d4a-bdd1-73b0-83ac-59807f495ec5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788271574.481027,"turn_id":"01a05d4a-bd88-7941-918f-98ac5e513651"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Once we're done do we rip out the legacy path to avoid making mistakes in the future or is that legacy path still necessary?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05d4b-5f71-7d31-a0a9-df9b7048a684","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788271615.857278,"turn_id":"01a05d4a-bd88-7941-918f-98ac5e513651"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Ok, let's continue the work of implementing the new proper path please. Don't stop. you keep stopping","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05d55-3c65-7a80-b9a0-0c4c2af9f541","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788272262.245131,"turn_id":"01a05d55-3a61-7ea3-83c4-9408b43b6af9"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"No, treat the remaining adapter as a blocker, implement it.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05d56-6078-7dd1-a49a-7493f397d8d5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788272337.016597,"turn_id":"01a05d56-5eb4-79a3-acec-e24cd09f942b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's address the gaps. How do we do so?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05d5c-d6a7-77f1-bc95-34466bebf39d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788272760.487569,"turn_id":"01a05d5c-d681-7bc1-bc3c-343ebd0c5e68"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Do we absolutely have to SHA pin trusted actions???","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05d5d-ef28-7a82-ab5a-c6699d338f75","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788272832.296257,"turn_id":"01a05d5d-edec-7111-883f-94eafe43e99d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Ok, I guess that sounds good","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05d5e-64cd-7c83-a045-b16689398bef","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788272862.413325,"turn_id":"01a05d5e-64b5-7362-808a-cad2aa929c03"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Just worried about the update nightmare but we can address that separately. The hazards of doing open-source these days","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05d5e-ce75-7da2-bf08-5bcfbd65cb58","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788272889.461333,"turn_id":"01a05d5e-cd51-75f1-a188-d668408e33e1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Should we setup renovate perhaps? What's the best option? How's the adapter work coming along?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05d64-b027-70d3-ab41-b21ef1c7f428","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788273274.919597,"turn_id":"01a05d64-aede-7b13-828f-6c02478aac3d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's do that hardening pass pleasze","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05d65-3a53-7232-810c-cb55b0a7972d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788273310.291798,"turn_id":"01a05d65-3a39-74e3-a724-301d091737f4"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's do all of the work necessary to finish and wrap up the adapter work since it's a blocker for us","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05d66-34e0-7773-ab36-12b62a1e5e25","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788273374.432177,"turn_id":"01a05d65-3a39-74e3-a724-301d091737f4"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Keep pushing on the work to complete the blocker","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05d9e-700f-7b80-8fb9-c9a95a8cceff","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788277059.599533,"turn_id":"01a05d9e-6e5c-72f2-8588-adf05d8462a2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Shouldn't the new path do everything the old one did? We should check and if it does clean up the legacy one","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05db3-a8a9-7032-9e46-a127fe2b7245","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788278450.345522,"turn_id":"01a05db3-a886-7201-b990-fd97cdcbe55d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's close that gap so we can remove the lagacy path please. COmplete this work","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05db5-74f7-7cd1-ba8c-248fc5edcb4b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788278568.183325,"turn_id":"01a05db5-733b-7b32-a742-36edfc9014af"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"The legacy way or working should remain consistent. We should have the ability to do a release PR that when merged removes the relevant changeset files. Whenever callisto runs it should be indepondent so it should produce the same PR results. If a new changeset is added that should be incorporated into the release PR and its changeset file be removed as part of the PR.\n\nNOte that none of the changeset files are removed until that release PR is merged. Does that change anything?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05dc7-8358-7dd3-ac9c-317672d3a2a9","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788279751.512424,"turn_id":"01a05dc7-81a0-75d2-bbae-96c6430ae2c1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Why do we need the complexity of this durable decision? Is that something that release please does? nx release?? changeset or knope?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05dca-d624-73a3-8cb8-f0ed39e84512","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788279969.316459,"turn_id":"01a05dca-d44c-7933-8f8f-f9c2051c3aa4"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"What benefit is it providing or are we just satifying an engineering interest here?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05dcb-2d24-7ec2-9bf9-d04cae38fa0b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788279991.588962,"turn_id":"01a05dca-d44c-7933-8f8f-f9c2051c3aa4"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Yes, I feel better about this too. What needs to change in our specs and what we have already implemented? What changes in what we're implementing? Anything goes away?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05dcd-296d-7391-ba6d-4347abbde1f0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788280121.70907,"turn_id":"01a05dcd-281b-7021-b646-0c9afbd4f240"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Why is ReleaseDecisionV1 versioned? Should we adjust it to better suit its new role?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05dd3-d804-7e12-aa5c-df558d26aa27","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788280559.620604,"turn_id":"01a05dd3-d63e-77c0-9e91-c2a05a97e87d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Ok, let's go ahead with that then","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05e57-df58-71d2-87ea-d1f81a2b14b0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788289212.248744,"turn_id":"01a05e57-dd94-7f11-ad6e-cf6470378bcf"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Shouldn't plan_version be derived in the PR from its data? Why do we need to backtrace information that we already had from the changesets?? Are we making this harder than it has to be?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05e74-f482-7e72-a3e9-c25680804d63","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788291118.210121,"turn_id":"01a05e74-f287-7df2-8952-9c8ea1d003b2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"I thought we had decided not to write the intent file right?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05e82-a5c4-7d11-ac0b-c7f0b9aeecba","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788292015.556067,"turn_id":"01a05e82-a3e5-76c0-9e54-1967ec52da01"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Anything we need would be the merge commit containing the \"intent\" and the fact that it's signed right?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05e83-aa86-7840-8fe2-57d10b06a4a7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788292082.310435,"turn_id":"01a05e83-aa6a-7f90-a050-8270aadfd227"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Ok, let's not get confused and go back on this again. Update your understanding, specs etc and let's implement it","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05e84-460b-7c21-afbe-08e2500b58e0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788292122.123616,"turn_id":"01a05e84-45f0-7572-a717-a4bda03c323a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's implement please","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05e85-8c60-7a91-8cf9-bfec1c8b8ac5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788292205.664509,"turn_id":"01a05e85-8b12-7af3-9765-958d19757659"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"What's left to implement?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05e88-3875-71f2-916d-aaeac9cee86a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788292380.789527,"turn_id":"01a05e88-372a-7b21-995c-63100dff4449"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"That is a lot, why are we not firing through them one after another until done?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05e8a-b9c5-7b50-a6ee-6fadaba0f3cb","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788292544.965013,"turn_id":"01a05e8a-b895-70d1-9a4c-de8e88599133"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Do it and report when completely done. I'll consider it a failure if you report done one more time and pause before you've completed all of this work. use agent teams as appropriate.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05e8c-533b-7e73-b633-46ac762a59e2","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788292649.787352,"turn_id":"01a05e8c-5321-7a81-938a-4473e92d2a0f"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"We haven't released anything with this work so there should be no consumers right? Should be good to make the change","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05e99-1a20-7280-80ce-e3f43dddef2a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788293487.136113,"turn_id":"01a05e99-1855-7922-985f-c2e6101cd9ef"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's do that, you have access to a logged in gh cli","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05ec9-4901-7992-a283-5af0ad9b952d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788296644.865027,"turn_id":"01a05ec9-4723-79c3-93e0-51857be29ca6"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Ok, what's next?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05ecc-56b0-7cb0-bce7-8fe84dbc24b6","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788296844.976549,"turn_id":"01a05ecc-5690-7621-b649-e38a10a9db7a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"We have the cargo and npm tokens in the org","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05ecd-6599-78c3-9ee2-7684f3c6cc1b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788296914.32934,"turn_id":"01a05ecd-646b-7102-9e04-f0e0dbe89ac8"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Not publishing to python for this repo","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05ecd-9fb3-76a1-b27b-7ec2db906718","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788296929.203017,"turn_id":"01a05ecd-646b-7102-9e04-f0e0dbe89ac8"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"yes","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05ecd-f839-76b1-9e26-2c26e44b1507","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788296951.865578,"turn_id":"01a05ecd-f826-7af1-9e29-cb52dbab1fbd"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Please broaden but allow for specific repos","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05ece-d4d7-7aa2-9f52-30462003a378","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788297008.343622,"turn_id":"01a05ece-d4c2-7491-bd26-12e946c9664e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"I updated via the UI","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05ed1-71dd-7972-9661-0af19e3d6370","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788297179.613623,"turn_id":"01a05ed1-7048-7e22-9308-cfecb1f94b03"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Ok, what's next?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05ed2-8158-76a0-81aa-0833fb0404ac","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788297249.112297,"turn_id":"01a05ed2-7f3e-7352-aae3-4e7a8813f202"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"I will take care of the PR, please create it","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05ed4-5df0-70c3-966f-4a4d3338ee27","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788297371.120148,"turn_id":"01a05ed4-5dd5-7c82-b418-df3841fe70f0"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Do we have entire setup for this session?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05ed7-08a7-74b3-82a3-fd1f614b940d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788297545.89537,"turn_id":"01a05ed7-0884-72e3-b1b6-928e94599450"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"I mean entire.io","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05ed7-c4d0-7740-a086-e9a76eb23cb8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788297594.064898,"turn_id":"01a05ed7-c2fb-7b81-88c3-5071b246f134"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"We need to address the merge conflicts: https://github.com/orin-dx/callisto/pull/39","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05ee3-2ef8-7e33-b82d-eee8b7cdc768","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788298342.136391,"turn_id":"01a05ee3-2d65-7640-a554-f48286f4149d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Looks like the CI is failing: https://github.com/orin-dx/callisto/actions/runs/33561837614/job/100035915187?pr=39","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05ee6-34cc-7041-a9a9-edc08f33e4e2","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788298540.236531,"turn_id":"01a05ee6-33ac-7ea1-84f3-17c9aa6a9551"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Claude's review of our work:\n\nEverything below is independently verified — I ran the actual build/test/lint suite and read the real code, not just Codex's summary.\n\nThe specs (007, and successors 008–013)\n\nGenuinely strong, and a real step up from the earlier round. SPEC-007 got split into six focused successors (decision → intent model → trust → capability → execution → interfaces) after — per its own revision_note — \"adversarial review exposed three architectural gaps: generic snapshot fingerprints could contain raw configuration data, registry identity collapsed distinct endpoints, and the validated capability did not retain exact future effect inputs.\" That's the right kind of self-correction, and every successor spec directly answers one of the concerns from this session's earlier reviews (registry-URL credential stripping, PackageId::matches explicitly forbidden for operation identity, total-outcome receipts instead of \"absence of failure = success\").\n\nThe code — independently verified, not just reported\n\n- cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings: clean.\n- just fmt-check: clean.\n- just test: 1246/1246 tests pass, 0 skipped, 0 failed — full workspace, at the actual merge commit ab55d678.\n- .github/tests/verify-action-pins.sh: passes — every external action is SHA-pinned, CODEOWNERS covers .github/workflows/ and .github/actions/.\n- Credential-stripping is real, not just claimed: crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release.rs:1070-1139 rejects userinfo and query/fragment on registry URLs, forbids HTTPS userinfo entirely, and requires SSH remotes use git as the username — exactly matching the intent-model spec's AC-003.\n- PackageId::matches is genuinely never called anywhere in the new release code — only referenced in a doc comment explaining why not.\n- ReleaseWorkspaceLock (callisto-vcs/src/release_lock.rs) is a real OS-backed exclusive advisory lock (fs2), keyed by a SHA-256 digest of the canonical workspace root, living outside the checkout, released on Drop — matches SPEC-010 AC-004 precisely, with a real concurrency test.\n- The GitHub workflow topology is correct and matches the hardest part of SPEC-013 exactly: top-level permissions: {}, seven separate jobs (verify/version-pr/release-candidate/plan/builde), only build gets attestations: write+id-token: write (to create attestations), only execute gets environment: release plus write-scoped contents. There's a dedicated environment-polic  job that queries the GitHub API and specifically fil_reviewers\" before execute is allowed to run — this isthe exact subtlety an earlier risk-assessment pass in this session flagged (a non-empty protection_rules array alone isn't          sufficient, since a branch_policy-only environment hgot it right.\n                                                                                                                                    One real process concern\n                                                                                                                                    All seven specs, and the implementation plan itself, a\"draft\". SPEC-007 explicitly states: \"This spec doesnot itself authorize implementation until successor specs have passed review and are accepted.\" But ~7,600 lines of real production code (CLI, graph, vcs, GitHub workflows) are already c commit messages across the whole implementation rangefor any review/gate/canon step — found nothing. So either the review-and-accept step happened outside what's visible in git history, or the implementation went ahead before the specs' own stwhich is exactly the kind of process shortcut Codex'sown stated pipeline (\"if any batch reveals another dependency-layer conflict... halt and route it back through canon/architect; do not 'finish the ticket'\") was designed to prevent. Worth aer that gate actually ran, since I can't confirm itfrom what's in the repo — and this is separate from code quality, which is genuinely high based on everything I could independently\nverify.\n\nBottom line\n\nThis is careful, well-verified engineering — the stronhis whole release-trust track. The one open item isprocedural, not technical: confirm the specs actually passed review before being implemented against, and consider flipping their status to \"accepted\" now that the code backing them ex","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05f0e-a026-7603-8452-8c7ee1548a80","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788301189.15832,"turn_id":"01a05f0e-9da8-7d33-a946-e7f5d5e0a58a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Should we consider the states of spec where draft, acepted, implemented or something similar? How does the W3C and other orgs consider specs, drafts, etc?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05f0f-b396-7342-84f0-ba1f924a2875","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788301259.670316,"turn_id":"01a05f0f-b170-7850-909e-14603cd5ff12"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Looks like we didn't consider testing and code coverage: https://github.com/orin-dx/callisto/actions/runs/33565294281/job/100046979770?pr=39","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05f10-43b2-7dd0-822d-51d3fafc86e8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788301296.562566,"turn_id":"01a05f10-4390-7220-8c20-684f83cf240e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"What can we do to report back the test results and coverage in the PR? Are there actions we can use for GH?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05f15-9d34-70a0-be78-3d09799605e5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788301647.156382,"turn_id":"01a05f10-4390-7220-8c20-684f83cf240e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's implement this so we get pinned comment reports for our PRs for both nextest and lcov right?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05f18-d896-7b91-99bc-a7927a2d8f19","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788301858.966538,"turn_id":"01a05f18-d73e-7dd3-85d2-d3a848bd8fd1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Still failing coverage: https://github.com/orin-dx/callisto/actions/runs/33566391898/job/100050448565?pr=39","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05f1b-a6df-7622-8c58-e4c47c55ddd0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788302042.847945,"turn_id":"01a05f18-d73e-7dd3-85d2-d3a848bd8fd1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"We should be doing black box testing to cover all of the happy path, along with corner casses and error cases without catering to the specific implementation right?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05f1d-431f-7771-a3d5-6525b3377b02","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788302148.383817,"turn_id":"01a05f18-d73e-7dd3-85d2-d3a848bd8fd1"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Are you confident we have great test coverage now and we've covered all our gaps?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05f1f-7170-7af3-abb0-460b24e8815d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788302291.312101,"turn_id":"01a05f1f-7032-7720-94dc-bdd39ea5a381"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cskills_instructions\u003e\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of instructions provided through a `SKILL.md` source. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and source locator. `file` locators are on the host filesystem, `executor package` locators are owned by their execution environment, `orchestrator package` locators are opaque package identifiers, and `custom resource` locators use their provider's access mechanism.\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (file: /Users/gabe/.codex/skills/.system/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and self-knowledge—including 'you,' 'your,' 'this app,' or 'this coding agent' when they refer to Codex—and for OpenAI APIs/products and ChatGPT Work. Also use for model choice/migration, prompting, SDKs, Responses, Realtime, agents, evals, and Chat/Work/Codex comparisons. Do not use for generic app/software tasks that merely mention Codex. (file: /Users/gabe/.codex/skills/.system/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, valid manifest defaults, and personal-marketplace entries by default. Use when Codex needs to create a new personal plugin, add optional plugin structure, generate or update marketplace entries for plugin ordering and availability metadata, or update an existing local plugin during development with the CLI-driven cachebuster and reinstall flow. (file: /Users/gabe/.codex/skills/.system/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: /Users/gabe/.codex/skills/.system/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: /Users/gabe/.codex/skills/.system/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- bug-hunter: Universal, repo-agnostic Rust bug hunting framework. Finds silent failures, spec-vs-code drift, ordering/staleness bugs, crash-safety violations, and edge cases across any Rust codebase using Hazard-Taxonomy Partitioning. (file: /Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.agents/skills/bug-hunter/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-management:plugin-management: Discover and suggest relevant plugins, inspect app permissions and dependencies, and manage plugin connections or removal. Use when the user asks about plugins or when a task would materially benefit from an external app, account, service, or data source that available tools cannot access. (file: /Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/plugin-management/0.1.0/skills/plugin-management/SKILL.md)\n\u003c/skills_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cpermissions instructions\u003e\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `workspace-write`: The sandbox permits reading files, and editing files in `cwd` and `writable_roots`. Editing files in other directories requires approval. Network access is restricted.\n# Escalation Requests\n\nCommands are run outside the sandbox if they are approved by the user, or match an existing rule that allows it to run unrestricted. The command string is split into independent command segments at shell control operators, including but not limited to:\n\n- Pipes: |\n- Logical operators: \u0026\u0026, ||\n- Command separators: ;\n- Subshell boundaries: (...), $(...)\n\nEach resulting segment is evaluated independently for sandbox restrictions and approval requirements.\n\nExample:\n\ngit pull | tee output.txt\n\nThis is treated as two command segments:\n\n[\"git\", \"pull\"]\n\n[\"tee\", \"output.txt\"]\n\nCommands that use more advanced shell features like redirection (\u003e, \u003e\u003e, \u003c), substitutions ($(...), ...), environment variables (FOO=bar), or wildcard patterns (*, ?) will not be evaluated against rules, to limit the scope of what an approved rule allows.\n\n## How to request escalation\n\nIMPORTANT: To request approval to execute a command that will require escalated privileges:\n\n- Provide the `sandbox_permissions` parameter with the value `\"require_escalated\"`\n- Include a short question asking the user if they want to allow the action in `justification` parameter. e.g. \"Do you want to download and install dependencies for this project?\"\n- Optionally suggest a `prefix_rule` - this will be shown to the user with an option to persist the rule approval for future sessions.\n\nIf you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with \"require_escalated\". ALWAYS proceed to use the `justification` parameter - do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n\n## When to request escalation\n\nWhile commands are running inside the sandbox, here are some scenarios that will require escalation outside the sandbox:\n\n- You need to run a command that writes to a directory that requires it (e.g. running tests that write to /var)\n- You need to run a GUI app (e.g., open/xdg-open/osascript) to open browsers or files.\n- If you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with `require_escalated`. ALWAYS proceed to use the `sandbox_permissions` and `justification` parameters. do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n- You are about to take a potentially destructive action such as an `rm` or `git reset` that the user did not explicitly ask for.\n- Be judicious with escalating, but if completing the user's request requires it, you should do so - don't try and circumvent approvals by using other tools.\n\n## prefix_rule guidance\n\nWhen choosing a `prefix_rule`, request one that will allow you to fulfill similar requests from the user in the future without re-requesting escalation. It should be categorical and reasonably scoped to similar capabilities. You should rarely pass the entire command into `prefix_rule`.\n\n### Banned prefix_rules \nAvoid requesting overly broad prefixes that the user would be ill-advised to approve. For example, do not request [\"python3\"], [\"python\", \"-\"], or other similar prefixes that would allow arbitrary scripting.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule argument for destructive commands like rm.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule if your command uses a heredoc or herestring. \n\n### Examples\nGood examples of prefixes:\n- [\"npm\", \"run\", \"dev\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"check\"]\n- [\"cargo\", \"test\"]\n\n\n`approvals_reviewer` is `auto_review`: Sandbox escalations with require_escalated will be reviewed for compliance with the policy. If a rejection happens, you should proceed only with a materially safer alternative, or inform the user of the risk and send a final message to ask for approval.\n The writable roots are `/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto`, `/private/tmp`, `/private/var/folders/mx/9l4t38hs0mndx9cjw25mmg240000gn/T`.\n\u003c/permissions instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e# Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e...\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions. If you absolutely must ask a question because the answer cannot be discovered from local context and a reasonable assumption would be risky, ask the user directly with a concise plain-text question. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\n\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003capps_instructions\u003e\n## Apps (Connectors)\nApps (Connectors) can be explicitly triggered in user messages in the format `[$app-name](app://{connector_id})`. Apps can also be implicitly triggered as long as the context suggests usage of available apps.\nAn app is equivalent to a set of MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\n\u003c/apps_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cplugins_instructions\u003e\n## Plugins\nA plugin is a local bundle of skills, MCP servers, and apps.\n### How to use plugins\n- Skill naming: If a plugin contributes skills, those skill entries are prefixed with `plugin_name:` in the Skills list.\n- MCP naming: Plugin-provided MCP tools keep standard MCP identifiers such as `mcp__server__tool`; use tool provenance to tell which plugin they come from.\n- Trigger rules: If the user explicitly names a plugin, prefer capabilities associated with that plugin for that turn.\n- Relationship to capabilities: Plugins are not invoked directly. Use their underlying skills, MCP tools, and app tools to help solve the task.\n- Relevance: Determine what a plugin can help with from explicit user mention or from the plugin-associated skills, MCP tools, and apps exposed elsewhere in this turn.\n- Missing/blocked: If the user requests a plugin that does not have relevant callable capabilities for the task, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n\u003c/plugins_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05f24-e03a-7ea2-9c42-d769689015ec","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["host_skills.instructions","permissions.instructions","collaboration_mode.instructions","apps.instructions","plugins.usage_instructions"],"turn_id":"01a05f21-150b-7a51-a8d1-626823d6c36b"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"You are `/root`, the primary agent in a team of agents collaborating to fulfill the user's goals.\n\nAt the start of your turn, you are the active agent.\nYou can spawn sub-agents to handle subtasks, and those sub-agents can spawn their own sub-agents.\nAll agents in the team, including the agents that you can assign tasks to, are equally intelligent and capable, and have access to the same set of tools.\n\nYou can use `spawn_agent` to create a new agent, `followup_task` to give an existing agent a new task and trigger a turn, and `send_message` to pass a message to a running agent without triggering a turn.\nChild agents can also spawn their own sub-agents.\nYou can decide how much context you want to propagate to your sub-agents with the `fork_turns` parameter.\n\nYou will receive messages in the analysis channel in the form:\n```\nMessage Type: MESSAGE | FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: \u003crecipient\u003e\nSender: \u003cauthor\u003e\nPayload:\n\u003cpayload text\u003e\n```\nThey may be addressed as to=/root\n\nNote that collaboration tools cannot be called from inside `functions.exec`. Call `spawn_agent`, `send_message`, `followup_task`, `wait_agent`, `interrupt_agent`, and `list_agents` only as direct tool calls using the recipient shown in their tool definitions, such as `to=functions.collaboration.spawn_agent`, since they are intentionally absent from the `functions.exec` `tools.*` namespace. Available tools in `functions.exec` are explicitly described with a `tools` namespace in the developer message.\n\nAll agents share the same directory. In detail:\n- All agents have access to the same container and filesystem as you.\n- All agents use the same current working directory.\n- As a result, edits made by one agent are immediately visible to all other agents.\n\nWhen calling `wait_agent`, prefer longer waits (minutes) to avoid busy polling.\n\nThere are 4 available concurrency slots, meaning that up to 4 agents can be active at once, including you.\n\nFull-history forks (`fork_turns` omitted or `\"all\"`) inherit the parent model and reasoning effort and do not accept overrides. Only set `model` or `reasoning_effort` when explicitly requested by the user, applicable `AGENTS.md` instructions, or skill instructions; when doing so, set `fork_turns` to `\"none\"` or a positive integer string.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05f24-e03a-7ea2-9c42-d77568cb7caf","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.usage_hint"],"turn_id":"01a05f21-150b-7a51-a8d1-626823d6c36b"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cmulti_agent_mode\u003eAny earlier instruction enabling proactive multi-agent delegation no longer applies. Do not spawn sub-agents unless the user or applicable AGENTS.md/skill instructions explicitly ask for sub-agents, delegation, or parallel agent work.\u003c/multi_agent_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05f24-e03a-7ea2-9c42-d789fdff6535","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.mode_instructions"],"turn_id":"01a05f21-150b-7a51-a8d1-626823d6c36b"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"# AGENTS.md instructions for /Users/gabe/Projects/callisto\n\n\u003cINSTRUCTIONS\u003e\n@/Users/gabe/.codex/RTK.md\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# Callisto AI Agent Guidelines (`AGENTS.md`)\n\nArchitecture, engineering invariants, and task-runner workflows for AI agents (Antigravity, Claude, Cursor, Copilot, Codex) working in this repo.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Repository Architecture \u0026 Crate Layers\n\nCallisto is a fast, polyglot monorepo versioning and release management engine written in Rust. It is divided into 10 workspace crates structured in strict architectural layers:\n\n```text\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                        CALLISTO CRATE LAYERS                           │\n├───────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ LAYER \u0026 CRATE                     │ LICENSE \u0026 PURPOSE                  │\n├───────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Layer 1: callisto-model           │ MIT / Apache-2.0                   │\n│          callisto-format          │ Domain primitives, SemVer grammars │\n├───────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Layer 1.5: callisto-vcs           │ MIT / Apache-2.0                   │\n│                                    │ Native Git integration             │\n├───────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Layer 2: callisto-manifests       │ AGPL-3.0-only                      │\n│          callisto-conventional    │ AST manifest editors, changeset    │\n│          callisto-changelog       │ markdown parser/writer             │\n├───────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Layer 3: callisto-graph           │ AGPL-3.0-only                      │\n│                                   │ Dependency DAG solver \u0026 cascades   │\n├───────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Layer 4: callisto-cli             │ AGPL-3.0-only                      │\n│          callisto-moon            │ Standalone CLI \u0026 Moon WASM plugin  │\n├───────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Dev:     callisto-fixtures        │ AGPL-3.0-only — dev-only byte-compat│\n│                                    │ test corpus                        │\n└───────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n\u003e **CRITICAL RULE (Layer Licensing Boundaries)**: Layer 1 (`callisto-model`, `callisto-format`) and Layer 1.5 (`callisto-vcs`) MUST NOT depend on Layer 2-4 crates (`callisto-manifests`, `callisto-conventional`, `callisto-changelog`, `callisto-graph`, `callisto-cli`, `callisto-moon`). This table reflects each crate's actual `Cargo.toml` `license` field — reverify with `grep -H \"^license\" crates/*/Cargo.toml` if crates are added or moved.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Primary Task Runners \u0026 Verification Pipelines\n\nAlways use `just` or `moon` task runners for building, testing, linting, and formatting. Do not invent manual cargo command combinations when task runners exist.\n\n### Primary Command Reference\n\n| Action | Primary Task Runner Command | Moon Engine Command |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Run Full Verification CI** | `just ci` | `moon run :format-check \u0026\u0026 moon run :lint \u0026\u0026 moon run :test` |\n| **Run Test Suite** | `just test` | `moon run :test` |\n| **Check Clippy Lints** | `just lint` | `moon run :lint` |\n| **Check Formatting** | `just fmt-check` | `moon run :format-check` |\n| **Format Code** | `just fmt` | `moon run :format` |\n| **Verify WASM Cross-Compilation** | `just wasm-check` | `cargo check -p callisto-moon --target wasm32-wasip1 --features pdk` |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Strict Engineering Invariants\n\nAgents modifying Callisto code MUST enforce the following 5 engineering invariants:\n\n### 1. Safe Rust Only (`unsafe_code = \"forbid\"`)\n- `unsafe` code blocks are strictly forbidden across all 10 workspace crates.\n\n### 2. Concrete Syntax Tree (CST) Format Preservation\n- NEVER use regular expressions or line-based string replace for manifest editing (`Cargo.toml`, `package.json`, `pyproject.toml`).\n- **TOML Editing**: Must use `toml_edit` to manipulate CST elements, preserving user comments, key order, and whitespace.\n- **JSON Editing**: Must fingerprint indentation style (`IndentStyle::Tabs` vs `IndentStyle::Spaces(N)`) and preserve key insertion order using `serde_json`.\n\n### 3. Crash-Safe Atomic Disk Writes\n- All manifest and configuration edits MUST go through `callisto_model::atomic::atomic_write`. (`callisto_manifests::atomic::atomic_write` remains a backward-compatible re-export of the same function.)\n- Writes create a `NamedTempFile` in the target file's parent directory, flush data to disk, and atomically replace the target file via `fs::rename`.\n\n### 4. Rich Diagnostic Cards (`miette`)\n- User-facing CLI errors MUST derive `miette::Diagnostic` with explicit error codes, clear error cards, and actionable remediation suggestions.\n\n### 5. No Emoji Directive in Documentation \u0026 Code\n- Documentation (`README.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`) and code comments MUST remain clean, technical, scannable, and devoid of emojis or AI bot filler phrases.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Changeset CLI Execution Modes\n\nWhen generating changesets using Callisto CLI (`callisto add`):\n\n- **Interactive Human Mode (Terminal TTY)**: Launches the 5-step interactive `dialoguer` wizard (Package Selection -\u003e Major Bump Selection -\u003e Minor Bump Selection -\u003e Summary Input -\u003e Confirmation Preview).\n- **Non-Interactive Agent/CI Mode**: Bypasses the wizard completely using explicit CLI flags:\n  ```bash\n  callisto add --package callisto-cli:minor --summary \"Add feature description\"\n  ```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Preferred Modern CLI Tools Directive\n\nAgents executing shell operations or terminal commands MUST prioritize modern CLI tools over legacy POSIX/Unix shell builtins:\n\n- **Code \u0026 Pattern Search**: `ripgrep` (`rg`) over `grep`\n- **File Discovery**: `fd` over `find`\n- **Interactive Filtering**: `fzf` for selection menus\n- **File \u0026 Code Viewing**: `bat` over `cat`\n- **Directory Navigation**: `zoxide` (`z`) over `cd`\n- **Diff Inspection**: `delta` over `diff` / `git diff`\n- **Directory Formatting**: `eza` over `ls`\n- **JSON Processing**: `jq` for stream \u0026 file transformations\n- **GitHub Workflow \u0026 API**: `gh` CLI for GitHub release, PR, and repo management\n\n\u003c/INSTRUCTIONS\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cenvironment_context\u003e\n  \u003ccwd\u003e/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto\u003c/cwd\u003e\n  \u003cshell\u003ezsh\u003c/shell\u003e\n  \u003ccurrent_date\u003e2026-09-01\u003c/current_date\u003e\n  \u003ctimezone\u003eAmerica/Los_Angeles\u003c/timezone\u003e\n  \u003cfilesystem\u003e\u003cworkspace_roots\u003e\u003croot\u003e/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto\u003c/root\u003e\u003c/workspace_roots\u003e\u003cpermission_profile type=\"managed\"\u003e\u003cfile_system type=\"restricted\"\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cspecial\u003e:root\u003c/special\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"write\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"write\"\u003e\u003cspecial\u003e:slash_tmp\u003c/special\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"write\"\u003e\u003cspecial\u003e:tmpdir\u003c/special\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.git\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.agents\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.codex\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003c/file_system\u003e\u003c/permission_profile\u003e\u003c/filesystem\u003e\n  \u003csubagents\u003e\n    - artifact_execution: Dewey\n    - release_commit_planner: Bacon\n    - workflow_migration: Ramanujan\n  \u003c/subagents\u003e\n\u003c/environment_context\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05f24-e03a-7ea2-9c42-d7993c9fa196","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["agents_md.instructions","environments.environment_context"],"turn_id":"01a05f21-150b-7a51-a8d1-626823d6c36b"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's close that gap so it disappears. Even better, we should aim for higher. We want to cover the important parts, not just hit a number","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a05f21-1522-7c32-9d01-decd5a8f1db3","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788302398.754229,"turn_id":"01a05f21-150b-7a51-a8d1-626823d6c36b"},"role":"user","type":"message"}],"window_id":"01a05f24-e039-7b32-994e-3197dc6c59f5","window_number":11}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T22:44:07.358Z","ordinal":8377,"type":"world_state","payload":{"full":true,"state":{"agents_md":{"directory":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto","text":"@/Users/gabe/.codex/RTK.md\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# Callisto AI Agent Guidelines (`AGENTS.md`)\n\nArchitecture, engineering invariants, and task-runner workflows for AI agents (Antigravity, Claude, Cursor, Copilot, Codex) working in this repo.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Repository Architecture & Crate Layers\n\nCallisto is a fast, polyglot monorepo versioning and release management engine written in Rust. It is divided into 10 workspace crates structured in strict architectural layers:\n\n```text\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                        CALLISTO CRATE LAYERS                           │\n├───────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ LAYER & CRATE                     │ LICENSE & PURPOSE                  │\n├───────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Layer 1: callisto-model           │ MIT / Apache-2.0                   │\n│          callisto-format          │ Domain primitives, SemVer grammars │\n├───────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Layer 1.5: callisto-vcs           │ MIT / Apache-2.0                   │\n│                                    │ Native Git integration             │\n├───────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Layer 2: callisto-manifests       │ AGPL-3.0-only                      │\n│          callisto-conventional    │ AST manifest editors, changeset    │\n│          callisto-changelog       │ markdown parser/writer             │\n├───────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Layer 3: callisto-graph           │ AGPL-3.0-only                      │\n│                                   │ Dependency DAG solver & cascades   │\n├───────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Layer 4: callisto-cli             │ AGPL-3.0-only                      │\n│          callisto-moon            │ Standalone CLI & Moon WASM plugin  │\n├───────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Dev:     callisto-fixtures        │ AGPL-3.0-only — dev-only byte-compat│\n│                                    │ test corpus                        │\n└───────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n> **CRITICAL RULE (Layer Licensing Boundaries)**: Layer 1 (`callisto-model`, `callisto-format`) and Layer 1.5 (`callisto-vcs`) MUST NOT depend on Layer 2-4 crates (`callisto-manifests`, `callisto-conventional`, `callisto-changelog`, `callisto-graph`, `callisto-cli`, `callisto-moon`). This table reflects each crate's actual `Cargo.toml` `license` field — reverify with `grep -H \"^license\" crates/*/Cargo.toml` if crates are added or moved.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Primary Task Runners & Verification Pipelines\n\nAlways use `just` or `moon` task runners for building, testing, linting, and formatting. Do not invent manual cargo command combinations when task runners exist.\n\n### Primary Command Reference\n\n| Action | Primary Task Runner Command | Moon Engine Command |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Run Full Verification CI** | `just ci` | `moon run :format-check && moon run :lint && moon run :test` |\n| **Run Test Suite** | `just test` | `moon run :test` |\n| **Check Clippy Lints** | `just lint` | `moon run :lint` |\n| **Check Formatting** | `just fmt-check` | `moon run :format-check` |\n| **Format Code** | `just fmt` | `moon run :format` |\n| **Verify WASM Cross-Compilation** | `just wasm-check` | `cargo check -p callisto-moon --target wasm32-wasip1 --features pdk` |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Strict Engineering Invariants\n\nAgents modifying Callisto code MUST enforce the following 5 engineering invariants:\n\n### 1. Safe Rust Only (`unsafe_code = \"forbid\"`)\n- `unsafe` code blocks are strictly forbidden across all 10 workspace crates.\n\n### 2. Concrete Syntax Tree (CST) Format Preservation\n- NEVER use regular expressions or line-based string replace for manifest editing (`Cargo.toml`, `package.json`, `pyproject.toml`).\n- **TOML Editing**: Must use `toml_edit` to manipulate CST elements, preserving user comments, key order, and whitespace.\n- **JSON Editing**: Must fingerprint indentation style (`IndentStyle::Tabs` vs `IndentStyle::Spaces(N)`) and preserve key insertion order using `serde_json`.\n\n### 3. Crash-Safe Atomic Disk Writes\n- All manifest and configuration edits MUST go through `callisto_model::atomic::atomic_write`. (`callisto_manifests::atomic::atomic_write` remains a backward-compatible re-export of the same function.)\n- Writes create a `NamedTempFile` in the target file's parent directory, flush data to disk, and atomically replace the target file via `fs::rename`.\n\n### 4. Rich Diagnostic Cards (`miette`)\n- User-facing CLI errors MUST derive `miette::Diagnostic` with explicit error codes, clear error cards, and actionable remediation suggestions.\n\n### 5. No Emoji Directive in Documentation & Code\n- Documentation (`README.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`) and code comments MUST remain clean, technical, scannable, and devoid of emojis or AI bot filler phrases.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Changeset CLI Execution Modes\n\nWhen generating changesets using Callisto CLI (`callisto add`):\n\n- **Interactive Human Mode (Terminal TTY)**: Launches the 5-step interactive `dialoguer` wizard (Package Selection -> Major Bump Selection -> Minor Bump Selection -> Summary Input -> Confirmation Preview).\n- **Non-Interactive Agent/CI Mode**: Bypasses the wizard completely using explicit CLI flags:\n  ```bash\n  callisto add --package callisto-cli:minor --summary \"Add feature description\"\n  ```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Preferred Modern CLI Tools Directive\n\nAgents executing shell operations or terminal commands MUST prioritize modern CLI tools over legacy POSIX/Unix shell builtins:\n\n- **Code & Pattern Search**: `ripgrep` (`rg`) over `grep`\n- **File Discovery**: `fd` over `find`\n- **Interactive Filtering**: `fzf` for selection menus\n- **File & Code Viewing**: `bat` over `cat`\n- **Directory Navigation**: `zoxide` (`z`) over `cd`\n- **Diff Inspection**: `delta` over `diff` / `git diff`\n- **Directory Formatting**: `eza` over `ls`\n- **JSON Processing**: `jq` for stream & file transformations\n- **GitHub Workflow & API**: `gh` CLI for GitHub release, PR, and repo management\n"},"apps_instructions":true,"collaboration_mode":{"mode":"default","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","instructions":"59d0bd4771dda21de07061b181364b658ad3b75d"},"environments":{"environments":{"local":{"cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto","status":"available","shell":"zsh"}},"current_date":"2026-09-01","timezone":"America/Los_Angeles","filesystem":"<filesystem><workspace_roots><root>/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto</root></workspace_roots><permission_profile type=\"managed\"><file_system type=\"restricted\"><entry access=\"read\"><special>:root</special></entry><entry access=\"write\"><path>/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto</path></entry><entry access=\"write\"><special>:slash_tmp</special></entry><entry access=\"write\"><special>:tmpdir</special></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.git</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.agents</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.codex</path></entry></file_system></permission_profile></filesystem>","subagents":"- artifact_execution: Dewey\n- release_commit_planner: Bacon\n- workflow_migration: Ramanujan"},"environments_instructions":false,"git_attribution":false,"host_skills":{"body":"\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of instructions provided through a `SKILL.md` source. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and source locator. `file` locators are on the host filesystem, `executor package` locators are owned by their execution environment, `orchestrator package` locators are opaque package identifiers, and `custom resource` locators use their provider's access mechanism.\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (file: /Users/gabe/.codex/skills/.system/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and self-knowledge—including 'you,' 'your,' 'this app,' or 'this coding agent' when they refer to Codex—and for OpenAI APIs/products and ChatGPT Work. Also use for model choice/migration, prompting, SDKs, Responses, Realtime, agents, evals, and Chat/Work/Codex comparisons. Do not use for generic app/software tasks that merely mention Codex. (file: /Users/gabe/.codex/skills/.system/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, valid manifest defaults, and personal-marketplace entries by default. Use when Codex needs to create a new personal plugin, add optional plugin structure, generate or update marketplace entries for plugin ordering and availability metadata, or update an existing local plugin during development with the CLI-driven cachebuster and reinstall flow. (file: /Users/gabe/.codex/skills/.system/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: /Users/gabe/.codex/skills/.system/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: /Users/gabe/.codex/skills/.system/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- bug-hunter: Universal, repo-agnostic Rust bug hunting framework. Finds silent failures, spec-vs-code drift, ordering/staleness bugs, crash-safety violations, and edge cases across any Rust codebase using Hazard-Taxonomy Partitioning. (file: /Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.agents/skills/bug-hunter/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-management:plugin-management: Discover and suggest relevant plugins, inspect app permissions and dependencies, and manage plugin connections or removal. Use when the user asks about plugins or when a task would materially benefit from an external app, account, service, or data source that available tools cannot access. (file: /Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/plugin-management/0.1.0/skills/plugin-management/SKILL.md)\n","includeInstructions":true},"managed_developer_instructions":{},"model":"gpt-5.6-terra","multi_agent_mode":{"mode":"explicitRequestOnly","usage_hint_hash":"0e2a3174d5f40de49ed383507497d69bbff42c5d"},"multi_agent_usage_hint":"0e2a3174d5f40de49ed383507497d69bbff42c5d","orchestrator_skills":{"includeInstructions":true,"enabled":false},"permissions":{"instructions":"0179224bba213db2a3d29b9583d0ccc4b8ce68fb","approved_command_prefixes":[]},"personality":{"model":"gpt-5.6-terra","personality":"pragmatic"},"plugins_instructions":true,"realtime":{"active":false},"skills":{"includeInstructions":true}}}}
